From 53c51129767d8b673a422f3a88aaea327ee89987 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Sergey Fedorov <barracuda@macos-powerpc.org>
Date: Thu, 5 Nov 2026 00:37:09 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 428/429] parse: `async [move] |args| [-> T] body` is an async
 closure

futures-copy 0.5.0 (arti 2.7.0) src/copy_buf.rs:256
`MockRuntime::test_with_various(async |rt| { .. })`:
"Unexpected token TOK_PIPE, expected TOK_BRACE_OPEN". Async closures
(1.85) were never parsed: after `async` only `[move] { .. }` was known.

The closure parser takes `is_async` and makes the body an async block
(a non-block body goes into a block first), the AST closure records it
(`m_is_async`; the dumper prints the source form back, as an attribute
proc macro re-lexes it), and the HIR lowering moves the declared `-> T`
into the block's `m_return` (the future's output, as for an async fn)
and marks the block as a closure body.

For such a block annotate_value_usage moves the closure's own variables
(its arguments) into the future whatever the body does with them, since
the closure's frame is gone when the future runs, while every other
capture is used as the body uses it (rustc's rule). A plain
`|args| async move { body }` desugar would move every capture and make
`.map(async |addr| runtime.listen(addr).await)` (arti-relay relay.rs:295)
FnOnce; a non-move block would keep `&arg` into the dead frame.
`async move |..|` is a move closure with a move block: the future owns
everything. The macro `expr` fragment matcher accepts the form too
(tor-rtcompat `test_with_one_runtime!(async |rt| ..)`).

Assisted-by: Claude Fable 5.1
